    "landing_faq_6W7fCC": {
      "type": "landing-faq",
      "blocks": {
        "question_w6Us3f": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Is it actually a good gift for a dad?",
            "answer": "<p>For a dad who wants to do more with his kid but sometimes runs out of ideas? Absolutely. Instead of another mug announcing he’s the world’s greatest dad, give him a kit with 28 opportunities to make a case for the title.</p><p>You give the gift. We provide the plans. He takes all the credit.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_tpdmZF": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Is this actually useful, or just a gag gift?",
            "answer": "<p>It’s funny, but there’s a real plan inside. Each mission shows Dad how to get started, what he’ll need, and how to do the activity with his kid. This is a kit for actual use, not a joke that gets one laugh and retires to a shelf.</p><p>Humor included. Participation required. Dad competence optional.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_QLC7cN": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Will Dad actually read the book?",
            "answer": "<p>It’s written with humor at Dad’s expense, so there’s a decent chance he’ll read more than he intended. But he doesn’t need to finish the whole thing before using it.</p><p>One mission. A few instructions. Then the book goes down and Dad gets up. A surprisingly effective reading strategy.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_0GkKlu": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "What if Dad hates reading?",
            "answer": "<p>Perfect. He isn’t signing up for a book club. He can scan the mission details, pick one that works, read the instructions, and get going.</p><p>If reading doesn’t sell him on it, maybe the part where he’s encouraged to rip out the pages will.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_sE86nl": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Is it for new dads, veteran dads, stepdads, and weekend dads?",
            "answer": "<p>Yep. First-time dads, seasoned pros, stepdads, adoptive dads, foster dads, and dads sharing custody can all use it. Pick missions suited to your kid’s age and the time you have together.</p><p>Buying for a brand-new dad? It’s an activity kit, not a newborn-care manual. Some missions will be for later. His qualifications at folding paper boats won’t matter either way.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_PdpF2C": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "What kind of dad will love this?",
            "answer": "<p>The dad who likes being silly with his kid. The dad who wants to be that guy but doesn’t always know where to start. The dad who’s terrific at big plans and mysteriously short on ideas by Saturday at 10:17 a.m.</p><p>He doesn’t have to be naturally creative. He just has to be willing to show up and follow the plan. The dramatic pirate voice is optional. Technically.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_T7LY0N": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "What if Dad isn’t crafty or feels ridiculous doing this stuff?",
            "answer": "<p>Good. He’s qualified.</p><p>The missions come with instructions, so he doesn’t need to be an artist, engineer, or suspiciously perfect Instagram dad. Start with something comfortable and go from there.</p><p>If the paper boat is crooked, see if it floats. Your kid isn’t judging the craftsmanship. Well, not <em>officially</em>.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_pBfY86": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Can moms, grandmas, and grandpas do the missions too?",
            "answer": "<p>Absolutely. The book is written for dads, but moms, grandparents, and other grown-ups can join in. We’re not checking birth certificates before anyone builds a living-room fort.</p><p>Dad gets the title. Everyone can get in on the fun.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_BAc9wV": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "What ages is the kit for?",
            "answer": "<p>It’s designed for dads and kids at different stages, roughly ages 1–12. Not every mission is right for every age, so the book includes guidance to help you choose. With toddlers, Dad takes the lead. Older kids can help plan, make decisions, and occasionally inform Dad that his entire approach is wrong.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_HDBQkL": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Will my kid actually remember these missions?",
            "answer": "<p>We can’t promise which moments will stick. But doing something ridiculous together gives you a story, and the sticker-covered poster gives you a reason to tell it again. Remember the disastrous pancake masterpiece? The highly suspicious pirate treasure?</p><p>That’s the idea: more “Remember when we…” and fewer Saturdays nobody can remember.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_vuT7yr": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "What do Dad and kid actually get out of this?",
            "answer": "<p>Your kid gets Dad’s attention, a say in the mission, and someone willing to join the nonsense. Dad gets 28 ready-made plans instead of having to invent an entire childhood every Saturday morning.</p><p>Together they collect inside jokes, small victories, and stories that belong to them. Dad also gets physical evidence that he occasionally knows what he’s doing.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_QTrzRN": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Why is this better than buying another toy?",
            "answer": "<p><strong>Because Dad comes with it.</strong></p><p>A toy gives your kid something to play with. This kit gives Dad and kid 28 things to do <em>together</em>, with instructions to get the fun started and a poster to celebrate what they did.</p><p>Your kid probably owns enough toys. A chance to review Dad’s performance as a pirate? That’s new.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_2CqaAV": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Is this another parenting advice book?",
            "answer": "<p>Nope. It’s a book you <em>use</em>, not one that tells you everything you’re doing wrong. Inside are 28 guided missions. Pick one, tear out and unfold the page, follow the steps, and get into it with your kid.</p><p>We skipped the parenting lectures. The internet has that department fully staffed.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_ZSapzE": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How do the missions, stickers, and poster work together?",
            "answer": "<p>Pick a mission. Tear out and unfold its page. Do the activity together. When you’ve earned it, your kid awards you a premium gold-foiled trophy sticker, which goes on the Dad Achievement Poster.</p><p>Hang the poster where your kid can see it. Every sticker marks something you did together. Every empty space is another mission waiting to happen. Your kid might start requesting the next mission without you having to pitch it.</p><p>A reward system for Dad. Finally, someone understands management.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_Mr8P0k": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Can we do the missions more than once?",
            "answer": "<p>Of course. The page gets torn out once. The mission can happen as many times as you like. Keep the instructions and repeat a favorite, or change it up as your kid grows.</p><p>If your pirate career is going well, there’s no need to retire after one voyage.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_gaWZTD": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Will my kid even care about the poster?",
            "answer": "<p>The poster is a record of missions your kid helped complete, with shiny trophy stickers they get to award. Let them add the sticker, tell you their favorite part, and choose what to try next.</p><p>Hang it somewhere they’ll see it. There’s no guarantee every kid becomes a sticker fanatic, but seeing your shared wins can be a pretty good reason to ask, “Can we do another one?”</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_EsXUPJ": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Why are the pages meant to be torn out?",
            "answer": "<p>Because they’re mission sheets, not sacred manuscripts. Tear one out, unfold it, and bring the instructions wherever the action takes you. Keep it afterward if you want another round.</p><p>It’s one of the few books where missing pages mean you’re doing it right.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_0fj2hS": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How long do the missions take? What if Dad’s busy?",
            "answer": "<p>Some are quick activities, while others take more time or preparation. The mission guides help you check the time, supplies, and setup before choosing one that fits your day.</p><p>You don’t need to do them in order, and nobody expects all 28 by Sunday night. This is fatherhood, not a quarterly performance review.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_efwaut": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Is this going to make a huge mess?",
            "answer": "<p>Depends on the mission. Some leave almost no evidence. Others may require a towel, a protected table, and a convincing explanation for the other parent.</p><p>Check the supplies and setup first. Dad and kid can tackle the cleanup together, too. Apparently that’s also quality time. Terrible news.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_mbsKN6": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Why buy it now instead of someday?",
            "answer": "<p>Because “when things calm down” has a funny way of turning into next month, next year, and “Wait, when did you get so big?”</p><p>This isn’t about rushing through 28 missions. It’s about having a plan ready when you and your kid get an afternoon together. Start with one. Dad’s rise to glory can be gradual.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_sBAjm4": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "What does my kid get out of it?",
            "answer": "<p>A dad who’s ready to get involved. Your kid gets to explore, make decisions, celebrate completed missions, and see their wins on the poster.</p><p>The writing is for Dad. The experience belongs to both of them. And your kid gets to hand out the trophies, which is a frankly alarming amount of authority.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_3sxudz": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "What’s included in the kit?",
            "answer": "<p>The complete kit includes:</p><ul><li>A physical <strong>softcover</strong> book with <strong>28 tear-out, unfoldable missions</strong> and step-by-step instructions.</li><li><strong>28 premium gold-foiled trophy stickers</strong> to award for completed missions.</li><li>The <strong>Dad Achievement Poster</strong> to collect those trophies and display your shared wins.</li><li><strong>A letter from Santa</strong>, tucked into its special envelope.</li></ul><p>It’s a physical kit, not a download or a book of generic parenting tips. Dad glory comes in the package. Actual glory requires a little work.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_ybnf1o": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Why are the stickers important? Can I get extras?",
            "answer": "<p>The gold-foiled stickers are trophies, not filler. After a mission, your kid gets to award one and add it to the poster. That’s how an afternoon of silliness becomes a little ceremony, with a visible reminder of what you did together.</p><p>Want another child to have their own trophy wall? Check out the <a href=\"https://bestdadbook.com/products/epic-dad-achievement-poster-sticker-pack-bundle\">extra poster and sticker bundle</a> for availability.</p><p>One dad. Multiple performance evaluations.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_KNJNjy": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Do we need special tools or a trip to Home Depot?",
            "answer": "<p>Most missions use things you probably have around the house. Some need extra supplies or a bit of preparation, so each mission tells you what to check before starting.</p><p>Start with the kitchen drawers and the closet. If Dad still wants a trip to Home Depot, he’ll need a different excuse.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_cIFk71": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Are the missions safe?",
            "answer": "<p>Dad should choose activities appropriate for his child’s age and abilities, read the instructions, and supervise throughout. Anything involving water, cooking, tools, or small objects needs the right precautions.</p><p>If a mission doesn’t suit your kid or your space, adapt it safely or pick another. The Dad of the Year trophy is not worth a trip to the ER.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_xHETMO": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "What if my kid gets bored halfway through?",
            "answer": "<p>Start with a shorter mission. Let your kid make a decision or two. If an activity isn’t clicking, change it, take a break, or try a different one later.</p><p>You don’t have to complete all 28 in order. Even pirates take days off.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_AUuw9j": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Where is the book printed, how long is shipping, and what if I need to return it?",
            "answer": "<p>The book is <strong>printed in the EU</strong>. We ship internationally, including to the <strong>United States and Canada</strong>. Delivery times and shipping costs depend on the destination and are shown through our ordering process. See the <a href=\"https://bestdadbook.com/policies/shipping-policy\">Shipping Policy</a> for details. If you need the kit by a specific date, contact <a href=\"mailto:info@bestdadbook.com\">info@bestdadbook.com</a> before placing your order.</p><p><strong>Changed your mind?</strong> You have 30 days from delivery, sale purchases included. Email <a href=\"mailto:info@bestdadbook.com\">info@bestdadbook.com</a>, send the kit back unopened within 14 days, and we refund what you paid within 14 days of getting it back. No restocking fee. For US and Canadian orders, the return goes to Slovenia and the postage is on you. EU customers also have separate statutory withdrawal rights.</p><p><strong>Damaged or wrong item?</strong> We put it right and cover the return costs. See the full <a href=\"https://bestdadbook.com/policies/refund-policy\">Return Policy</a> for conditions, deadlines and exceptions.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
